@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the restriction on passing it on, examined
#
# The statement at pmc:TheRestrictionOnPassingItOn was formed from three
# witnesses and named its own falsifier: an operative text whose restriction on
# transmission stands only in an external rule, a colophon or a later
# ordinance, the working text itself silent. Since it was written the holding
# has taken in a dozen further transmissions that restrict their own passing
# on, and two runs left it a candidate witness without writing it in. This
# protocol puts the three clauses of the statement to all of them, and to the
# work harvested in the run of the same day, the Yasenkanna, whose preface
# describes a secrecy and its end.
#

pmp:RestrictionOnPassingItOnExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether transmissions that restrict their own passing on do it inside the operative text, name the recipient, and ground it in the text's authority"@en, "Ob Überlieferungen, die ihre eigene Weitergabe beschränken, das im operativen Text tun, den Empfänger nennen und es auf die Autorität des Textes gründen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the three-clause claim that transmissions of an operative text state the restriction on passing it on inside the text rather than in an external rule, name the class of permitted recipient, and attribute the restriction to the authority the text claims for its contents. The population is every node in the holding that states, reports or refuses a restriction on handing on a method, a book or a rite; drawn on 2026-10-16 by a whole-graph scan of the labels, definitions and notes of traditions/ for divulge, pass on, hand on, transmit only, worthy and unworthy, not to be shown, revealed, taught or given, keep secret, swear and withhold, which returned eighty-seven nodes; each was read at the node, and seventeen state a restriction on the passing on of their own operative content."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheRestrictionOnPassingItOn ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "For the first clause, as the statement itself names it: an operative text whose restriction on transmission stands only in an external rule, a colophon or a later ordinance, the working text silent. Not met in the strict form; one case comes close and is set out below. For the second: a restriction inside the text that names no class of recipient. Met once in a weak form. For the third: a restriction inside the text grounded in something other than the authority the text claims for its contents. Met four times, in two kinds, and the clause does not survive as worded."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched: the compiled holding as of 2026-10-16 on branch claude/ernte-yasenkanna, by the scan described in the definition, every candidate read at its node. The seventeen restrictions read: pmc:ArsNotoriaSecretumKautel, pmc:PgmIvTraditioClausa, pmc:PgmIvTraditioSubmissa, pmc:ShangqingMengjin, pmc:ShangqingHuangtingMengjin, pmc:ShangqingShieryanshi, pmc:AbramelinTraditioRestricta, pmc:IuratusIuramentumKautel, pmc:JeuMysteriaNonTradenda, pmc:RasarnavaGopaniyata, pmc:RasaGuptavidya, pmc:RosicrucianSecretumCentumAnnorum, pmc:TurbaCaveNeOstendas, pmc:AegyptiaArcanumAmenti, pmc:AgrippaSilentium, pmc:JapaneseZenHijuChinzo with pmc:JapaneseZenShuichiDaikantan, and, as a restriction reported and attacked by a critic, pmc:ZosimosSacerdotesNaturaliaOccultaverunt. Found against the third clause: Agrippa and the Rasa-jala-nidhi, the Rosicrucian Fama, the Liber iuratus. Found close to the falsifier of the first: the Yasenkanna. Not searched: the Sanskrit ritual manuals the statement's own note names as a likely place, beyond the Rasārṇava and the Rasa-jala-nidhi, because the holding's Vedic and Śaiva files carry no restriction node to read and no manual was opened for this protocol; and the grimoire literature beyond the four Solomonic and Honorian works in the holding."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-16" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-16" ;
    skos:note "The first clause holds as far as the holding reaches. In twelve of the thirteen operative works the population comes from, the restriction stands in the working text itself — in the mouth of the angel, the speaker of the rite, the master, the rubric or the closing chapter — and no case was found of a restriction that stands only in a colophon or an outside rule while the working text is silent. Abramelin is a fourth witness of the original kind and is now written in at the statement: a restriction in the operative text, a class of recipient named, permitted and excluded, and a ground in the grace the text claims, given free and abused by Solomon."@en ;
    skos:note "The case that comes closest to the falsifier, and why it does not meet it. The printed Yasenkanna lays no restriction on its reader at all. The secrecy it records lived outside the working text, in practice: the master taught the method in secret, the monks who copied the manuscript hid their copies and showed them to no one; and the preface, which is part of the printed work, tells how the secrecy ended, with the master's smile, at a bookseller's request (pmc:JapaneseZenHijuChinzo). What the working text carries is a restriction told as precedent and not laid on anyone, the master who would pass the elixir only to a vessel of the highest kind (pmc:JapaneseZenShuichiDaikantan). This is the falsifier's shape — a silent working text and a restriction outside it — without its substance, since what stands outside is a practice and not a rule, and since the text records its lifting rather than its force. One more fact keeps the case open: according to Waddell's note 70 the earlier version of the same story, in the Kanzanshi sendai kimon, has the hermit forbid reckless disclosure inside the text. If that reading holds, the restriction was in the working text of the manuscript version and was taken out of the printed one."@en ;
    skos:note "That last point is not singular, and it is the protocol's unexpected result. The restriction is recension-dependent in three transmissions of the population. Abramelin's French recension carries four clauses on transmission that the German third book, read complete, does not carry (note at pmc:AbramelinTraditioRestricta). The Book of the Dead clause admits father and son in Budge's text of chapter 137A and excludes them by name in Renouf's Turin text of chapter 161 (note at pmc:AegyptiaArcanumAmenti). And the Yasenkanna, if Waddell's note is right, drops the clause between manuscript and print. A statement about where the restriction stands is therefore a statement about a recension, and the population should be read recension by recension."@en ;
    skos:note "The second clause holds with one weak case. Every restriction inside a text names a class: the son, the worthy, the one known for a year, the one who passed twelve trials, the disciples, the pure-hearted. The Turba names only whom to exclude, the evil and kings; Agrippa names only those few wise men among whom the secrets pass by mouth. Both still draw a boundary by persons, so the clause bends rather than breaks; the wording is widened to permitted or excluded."@en ;
    skos:note "The third clause does not survive as worded. Two kinds of ground other than the authority the text claims were found. First, the claim that publication destroys the effect: Agrippa says every magical experiment flees the public, is strengthened by silence and destroyed by publication (pmc:AgrippaSilentium); the Rasa-jala-nidhi says the knowledge becomes powerful when cultivated in secret and that a medicine familiar to the patient and the public becomes ineffective (pmc:RasaGuptavidya). Here the restriction is grounded in the working of the art and not in its source, and both books are printed. Second, the ordinance of a community stated inside the text: the Liber iuratus gives its nine clauses as the oaths the masters swore in council (pmc:IuratusIuramentumKautel), and the Fama gives its secrecy as the sixth article of a fraternity, for a term of a hundred years (pmc:RosicrucianSecretumCentumAnnorum). These are inside the text and so do not touch the first clause, but they are an outside rule copied in, and the authority they invoke is the assembly's and not that which the text claims for its contents. The Turba stands between: one of its grounds is an oath by God and the master, two are about the holders, that the vulgar would laugh and that kings would take the thing away."@en ;
    skos:note "What the deviations cost the statement. The first clause stands and gains a witness. The second stands with its wording widened to the excluded class. The third is replaced by a disjunction that the cases support: the ground is the authority the text claims, or the claim that publication destroys the effect, or the ordinance of a community the text reproduces. Rewritten so at the node, with the old wording kept in a note. The disjunction is not empty: it fails on a restriction grounded in something else, for instance in the harm the method would do to outsiders, which is the one ground the Turba gives at Dictum 64 according to the note at its node and which this protocol did not read; that passage is the cheapest next case."@en ;
    skos:note "The cheapest next question is the Kanzanshi sendai kimon. It decides whether the holding has a case in which a restriction stood inside the working text of one version of an operative work and was removed from the printed version by its own author, which is the nearest thing to the falsifier any transmission in the holding has offered, and it is reachable once a readable text of Hakuin's Chinese writings is found (pmw:KanzanshiSendaiKimon)."@en .
